---
import { GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL } from '../consts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// One line per place keeps the page scannable; the résumé holds the full history.
const work = [
	{
		dates: '2026–present',
		org: 'Tufts Clinical and Translational Science Institute',
		what: [
			'I evaluate AI tools for development and data discovery. I also built a self-paced trainer that takes SQL analysts through OMOP data.',
			'OMOP is the common data model that research networks use to put EHR data from different hospitals into the same tables and codes.',
		],
	},
	{
		dates: '2023–2025',
		org: 'University of Illinois Chicago',
		what: ['I designed an OMOP data layer that put data from several EHR systems into one standard representation.'],
	},
	{
		dates: '2022',
		org: 'CDC',
		// COVID‑19 uses a non-breaking hyphen (U+2011) so the name never splits across two lines.
		what: [
			'I worked on federal and state data interoperability during COVID‑19 and contributed to USCDI data element proposals.',
		],
	},
	{
		dates: '2020–2023',
		org: 'Optum',
		what: [
			'I set the mapping standards for turning HL7 v2 messages into FHIR resources, and eight business units adopted them. I also led development of Patient Access APIs certified for USCDI v1.',
		],
	},
];
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="Jagnyesh Patra builds FHIR and OMOP pipelines and tests AI output against clinical data standards."
	narrow
>
	<article class="prose">
		<h1>About</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			I'm Jagnyesh Patra. Since 2019 I have worked on clinical and payer data at providers, a payer and its pharmacy
			benefit manager, and the CDC. Before that I held product and analyst roles in lending and telecom in Mumbai.
		</p>

		<h2>How I work</h2>
		<ul class="principles">
			<li>
				<p class="principle">I measure a claim against a baseline and publish the result even when it goes against me.</p>
				<p class="evidence">
					In <a href="/projects/#rcm-ai">rcm-ai</a>, the LLM-only baseline beat my knowledge graph on accuracy, and the
					README's results section says so.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p class="principle">I keep every fact traceable to the source row it came from.</p>
				<p class="evidence">In rcm-ai, every step behind an appealable verdict names its source row.</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p class="principle">I put human review where a wrong answer costs the most.</p>
				<p class="evidence">
					In <a href="/projects/#researchflow">researchflow</a>, no SQL query runs until an informatician approves it.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Where I've worked</h2>
		<ul class="work">
			{
				work.map((place) => (
					<li>
						<p class="meta">
							<span>{place.dates}</span>
						</p>
						<div>
							<p class="org">{place.org}</p>
							{place.what.map((sentence) => (
								<p class="what">{sentence}</p>
							))}
						</div>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ul>

		<h2>From product roles to writing the code</h2>
		<p>
			Most of that work was in product and analyst roles. I interviewed clinicians and principal investigators, wrote
			requirements, and launched MVPs. Now I write the code and the evaluations as well.
			The <a href="/projects/">projects</a> on this site show that work on synthetic and public data, never patient
			records.
		</p>

		<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
		<p>
			My <a href="/resume/">résumé</a> has the full work history. The code is on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>, and
			I'm on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>.
		</p>
	</article>
</Base>
<style>
	.lede {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}
	.principles,
	.work {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
		list-style: none;
		margin: 1rem 0 0;
		padding: 0;
	}
	.principle,
	.evidence {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.principle {
		font-weight: 500;
	}
	.evidence,
	.what {
		color: var(--secondary);
		font-size: 0.9375rem;
	}
	.work li {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.25rem;
	}
	.work p {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.what + .what {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}
	.org {
		font-weight: 500;
	}
	/* A rail of years down the left once there is room; on phones the years sit above each place. */
	@media (min-width: 40rem) {
		.work li {
			grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
			align-items: baseline;
			gap: 0 1rem;
		}
	}
</style>
